**`custom.css` — cleaned up and reorganised.** Was previously a flat,
uncommented list of rules including two invalid empty `color: ;`
declarations (harmless — browsers silently drop them — but dead weight)
and a `#main { background: red; }` rule that looked like an early
loading-test, left in. Rewritten with section comments (base / layout /
components / utilities), invalid declarations flagged rather than
guessed at, and — importantly — **`.is-hidden { display: none; }` was
missing entirely**. `single-event.php`'s whole "PHP renders everything,
CSS hides empty fields" design has been running without its actual hide
rule in place until this session. Confirm on next page load that empty
fields on `single-event.php` now genuinely disappear.

**Design boundary set for `single-event.php`:** one wrapping
`<div class="event-content-wrap">` added around all content (title
through event-details section), styled via `custom.css`:
```css
.event-content-wrap {
    max-width: 700px;
    margin: 0 auto;
}
```
Header/footer remain full-width (untouched, outside this wrapper).
Content column now capped and centred inside the full-width band.

**Field-level CSS hooks confirmed as deliberate convention.** Every
field's wrapper carries a class matching its schema key exactly
(`field-event_date`, `field-venue`, `field-booking`, etc.) — mirrors the
JSON dump's own field names, so HTML and data vocabulary stay in sync.
Agreed as the pattern going forward for any new template.

**Conditional display philosophy locked in:** PHP always retrieves and
renders every field ("full noise") — no `if (empty())` guards deciding
whether markup exists. Visibility is CSS's job exclusively
(`.is-hidden` for "no data," case-by-case `.field-x` targeting for
context-specific design choices, e.g. a future "featured card" hiding
fields it doesn't want regardless of whether they have data). The two
mechanisms are compatible and don't conflict — different reasons,
same visual outcome, no interference between them.

