## Conceptual ground covered (worth remembering, not code changes)

- **PHP templates never appear in Site Editor's GUI template list.**
  WordPress checks for a matching PHP file *first*; only falls through to
  block templates (Site Editor-managed) if no PHP file exists. Once
  `single-event.php` exists, template 60 (the old DB-based Site Editor
  template) becomes permanently inert — still visible in the GUI, never
  actually used. Worth deleting template 60 eventually to avoid future
  confusion; not urgent.
- **Redesigning `single-event.php`'s structure = editing the PHP file
  directly.** No GUI equivalent exists once a PHP template is in place.
  Confirmed as the expected, correct way forward — not a limitation.
- **Template-parts / includes discussed as a future refactor**, not
  applied yet. Rationale for deferring: real benefit appears once markup
  needs reuse across multiple templates (e.g. once a homepage grid needs
  the same "event card" display as `single-event.php`) — not needed while
  there's only one real consumer of this markup. Correctly parked as
  "later, triggered by actual duplication," not "never."
- **theme.json walked through conceptually.** Established as the source
  of every `var(--wp--preset--...)` value already in use
  (`settings.spacing`, `settings.color.palette` specifically) — GUI
  (Site Editor Styles panel) edits it, PHP/CSS consume its generated
  variables. Not yet reviewed in detail against the user's actual file
  contents — worth a follow-up pass to confirm what specific values
  `spacing--60` etc. resolve to, and what colour names are available for
  future use (e.g. a "low stock" red warning discussed hypothetically
  earlier, wanting to use the theme's real palette rather than an
  arbitrary hex value).
- **`style.css` vs `custom.css` roles clarified:** `style.css` is
  primarily declarative (the theme-header comment WordPress needs to
  recognise the child theme) — real styling work deliberately kept in
  `custom.css` instead, enqueued explicitly via `functions.php`. Not a
  WordPress requirement, just the convention being followed.

